This folder holds the code for the OpenJML LSP server.
It has these artifacts:
- openjml-lsp -- the executable that launches the LSP server
- build/openjml-lsp.jar -- the compiled server, which links in OpenJMLsrc,
the openjml tool itself

Typically an LSP-capable IDE will be given the path to openjml-lsp,
and execute that script to launch the server, which will then
communicate with the IDE using JSON over a System.in/System.out text
communication channel. This interaction is managed by some LSP library.

For VSCode, there are these additional artifacts:
- OpenJMLlsp/bin/vscode-extension and contents, particularly extension.js and openjml-*.vsix, that are
a VSCode extension making VSCode a LSP client to OpenJML's LSP server
- OpenJMLsrc/release-files/install-vscode-extension, which is made part of an
OpenJML release. It is a convenience executable that installs the VSCode extension in an installed
instance of VSCode.

There is also an extension (a.k.a. Eclipse plugin) that connects the Eclipse IDE to OpenJML.
The project OpenJMLUI contains the source for that plugin; it also connects to openjml-lsp.

To build this LSP libraryi, make -C OpenJMLlsp release 
Then, when 'make -C openjmlsrsc' release is run, the LSP material will be picked up and
included in the OpenJML reelase.
Building OpenJMLlsp  presumes that OpenJMLsrc is built; openjml-lsp uses the IAPI/API programmatic interface
into openjml.

There are tests for the LSP server in OpenJMLTest/lsp.
Run them using 'make -C OpenJMLlsp run-tests' or 'make -C OpenJMLTest/lsp run-tests'
